{"qid":"Q43292","names":["Sultanate of Bone"],"title":"Sultanate of Bone","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Sultanate of Bone (Bugis: Kerajaan Bone) was the dominant Bugis polity of South Sulawesi, with its capital at Watampone. Converted to Islam under Gowa's coercion in 1611, Bone achieved primacy when its prince Arung Palakka allied with the Dutch VOC to crush Gowa in the Makassar War (1666–1669), after which Bone controlled the regional political landscape. Dutch military interventions in 1824 and 1859 progressively dismantled its sovereignty, and the Netherlands formally ended the sultanate's independence after the Bone War of 1859; a ceremonial sultanate persisted until 1950, when it was absorbed into the Republic of Indonesia.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial extent in South Sulawesi is reasonably documented from Dutch colonial sources and Bugis lontara manuscripts, but precise boundaries over the 1512–1859 attestation window are imprecisely mapped.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Bone Regency","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bone_Regency"},{"label":"Britannica: Bone, Indonesia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Bone-Indonesia"}],"britannica":"topic/sultan-Islamic-title","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Sultan","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"La Uliyo Botoé","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1517,"end":1542,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"La Tenrirawe Bongkange","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1542,"end":1584,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"La Ica","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":158